00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
 If you're a developer-- start that.

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:03,480
 If you're a software developer, I

3
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:05,880
 want you to think of the craziest edge cases in the world.

4
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:08,000
 The one thing that you thought, oh, yeah, there's

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,880
 no way a user would navigate and navigate and navigate

6
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:12,960
 and end up in this kind of situation.

7
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:15,200
 I promise you, with everything that I have

8
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:17,000
 and everything that I hope, someone

9
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:18,080
 is going to report an issue.

10
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:21,640
 And it's going to be exactly that edge case that you never

11
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:22,600
 thought you'd offer.

12
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:24,400
 It doesn't even have to be something big.

13
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:27,720
 You don't even have to have a lot of users for edge cases

14
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:31,440
 that you didn't write for to exist.

15
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:32,440
 Usually when we build,

16
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:35,400
 we create a direct simple path, you know?

17
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:38,880
 And just before that, you know, the user fills a form,

18
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,280
 they click a button, and then they wait.

19
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,320
 But it sounds straightforward, but it's not.

20
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:46,240
 Real users don't always do that.

21
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:48,320
 Real users don't just always fill the form

22
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:50,360
 and click the button and wait.

23
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:53,700
 Real users double-click, like ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta.

24
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:54,540
 They click a lot.

25
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:56,480
 Real users open different tabs.

26
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:58,840
 Real users network drop halfway.

27
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:02,200
 Real users leave the page open for two hours,

28
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:03,960
 and they go and then they come back.

29
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:05,280
 And they're not doing anything strange.

30
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:07,700
 They are just being users.

31
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:09,760
 One of the most common edge cases in production

32
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:11,560
 is the duplicate submission.

33
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:13,340
 If, for example, in an online shop,

34
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:15,360
 someone clicks place order,

35
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:17,860
 and maybe the page feels a little slow,

36
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:19,020
 they click it again.

37
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:21,840
 Now our server gets two requests,

38
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,740
 and it creates two orders.

39
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:25,320
 And if we charge on each order,

40
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:26,640
 you can type the user twice.

41
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:28,240
 It doesn't even need a double-click.

42
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:30,720
 The phone can lose the network after the request was sent

43
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:32,040
 and the app can retry.

44
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:34,040
 Or they can refresh the page, and then the browser

45
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:36,720
 asks them if they want to send the form again and they say yes.

46
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:38,880
 Disabling the button after the first click

47
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:41,360
 helps with this, but it only covers the double-click part.

48
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:44,160
 It does nothing for like maybe a retry or a second tab.

49
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:48,000
 The fix that is common for this is using an idempotency key.

50
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:49,680
 Idempotence-- like that.

51
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:52,600
 Basically, an idempotency key puts things in place

52
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,480
 such that doing something twice has the same result,

53
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:58,680
 as doing it just once.

54
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:01,320
 Stripe's API uses the same idea

55
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:02,960
 with an idempotency key header.

56
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:06,120
 So it retried request doesn't create a second payment.

57
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:08,720
 Another common edge case in production is a race condition.

58
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:11,240
 A race condition is when two things happen at the same time.

59
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:13,640
 And your code just assume that they would happen

60
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:14,920
 one after the other.

61
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:17,160
 There was this researcher called Edgar

62
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:18,800
 that found one of these race conditions

63
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:21,240
 in Starbucks, scratch that data.

64
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:26,760
 was this researcher called Edgar that found one of these race conditions in Starbucks gift cards.

65
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:34,840
 So he had gift cards worth $15 in total, and he sensed the same balance transfer twice,

66
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:39,720
 at the same time, from two different sessions. Both transfers went through, and his card ended

67
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:45,160
 up with $20 between them. So each request checked the balance, saw that it was enough money, and

68
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:49,640
 moved it. Neither request was there for the other one to finish. The same thing can happen in your

69
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:55,880
 have. For example, in our online shop, say we have one item left in stock, two people click

70
00:02:55,880 --> 00:03:02,680
 buy in the exact same second, both requests check the stock, both see one item left, both create

71
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:08,520
 an order, and now we have sold an item that we do not have. What do we do about this? So if the two

72
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:12,760
 requests arrive at the same time, only one of them can take the last item. The other one gets zero

73
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:19,000
 rows updated, and that's how you know it's sold out. A lot of edge cases, situation, and production

74
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,200
 can also happen with names because we sometimes

75
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:25,240
 build names featuring like default names.

76
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:27,240
 Take the name O'Brien, for example,

77
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:28,800
 the apostrophe is the same character

78
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:31,000
 that starts an empty piece of text in SQL.

79
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,960
 So if your code glues the name straight into the query,

80
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:37,260
 the apostrophe ends the text le, and the query breaks.

81
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:40,080
 And even apostrophe can break your query by accident,

82
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:41,920
 it means that someone can type something

83
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:44,320
 after the apostrophe on purpose,

84
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:45,840
 and that will be SQL injection.

85
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:48,040
 In China system, the BBC wrote about a woman

86
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:49,280
 called Jennifer Null.

87
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:55,440
 When Jennifer Null tries to buy a plane ticket,

88
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:57,520
 most websites tell her that she has left

89
00:03:57,520 --> 00:03:59,200
 the sunning field blank.

90
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:00,560
 A sunning is Null.

91
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:03,800
 The website sees the name Null and treats it as empty.

92
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,540
 And yeah, so Null, apart from even being in a name situation,

93
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:10,160
 is also an edge case that catches almost everyone

94
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:12,380
 because Null means no value.

95
00:04:12,380 --> 00:04:15,240
 There was another story, I was told at a conference,

96
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:19,120
 where a security researcher got a personal license plate

97
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:21,000
 in California that said NOL.

98
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:22,880
 Like that was the license plate, NOL.

99
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:25,800
 Then he started getting parking tickets over $12,000

100
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:28,120
 in fine that were not his tickets.

101
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:32,220
 There were tickets where no plate number had been recorded.

102
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:35,960
 And in the system that process in this, it's like that.

103
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:38,240
 There were tickets where no plate number had been recorded.

104
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:40,440
 And in the system that processes the tickets,

105
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:42,840
 those were now linked to a plate called NOL.

106
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:45,100
 So all of them landed on his plates.

107
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:46,940
 So this is another null situation.

108
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:48,660
 Another one that is common, another thing

109
00:04:48,660 --> 00:04:50,780
 that can cause very interesting edge cases situation

110
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:52,660
 in production is leap years.

111
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:55,540
 Leap years are an edge case that will show up every four years,

112
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:57,980
 which is exactly why you get missed.

113
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:05,060
 On the 29th of February, 2019, Microsoft's cloud window

114
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:09,100
 as all had an outage because of a deep debug.

115
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:12,120
 Part of their system made security certificates

116
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:13,740
 that were valid for one year.

117
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:16,020
 And the system worked out the end dates

118
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:18,900
 by taking today's date and just adding one to the year.

119
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:21,180
 So on the 29th of February, 2019,

120
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:24,500
 that logic gave 29th of February, 2013.

121
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:25,400
 Sorry, scratch that.

122
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:27,660
 So on the 29th of February, 2012,

123
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:31,580
 that system, that logic gave 29th of February, 2013,

124
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:33,020
 and that date doesn't exist.

125
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:35,620
 So creating the certificate failed

126
00:05:35,620 --> 00:05:37,580
 and part of Azure went down with it.

127
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:40,220
 So yeah, this is something to think about as well,

128
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:41,220
 leap year box.

129
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:43,420
 Another hedge case that can cause issues

130
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:47,560
 time zone and daylight saving. There's also an edge case in production that a

131
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:50,620
 lot of people do not think about. In Estonia where I live, the clock changes

132
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:54,780
 twice in a year. On the 29th of March, the clocks in Tallinn

133
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:59,620
 jump from 3 a.m. straight to 4 a.m. So 3.30 that morning never happened. The

134
00:05:59,620 --> 00:06:06,020
 same way on the 25th of October, the clock went from 4 a.m. back to 3 a.m.

135
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:12,020
 So 3.30 happened twice. Now imagine our online shop sends a daily sales report

136
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:18,580
 at 3.30, time. On one day of the year, that job would not run. On another day of the year,

137
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:22,740
 it would run twice. So these are things to consider. Another common edge case is the Unicode

138
00:06:22,740 --> 00:06:27,020
 bug. Unicode is the edge case that breaks names with ascents and tone marks. A lot of

139
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:33,460
 your names have this. Take at the bio. At the bio, for example, you can see seven letters,

140
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:39,540
 but some functions count bytes, not letters, right? So in this case, you would have 13,

141
00:06:39,540 --> 00:06:44,020
 because letters like "a" and "o" take more than one byte each.

142
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:47,700
 So if we check the name must be at most 20 characters.

143
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:51,060
 If we check that with the wrong function, you can reject actual names.

144
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:54,740
 So the fix for this is usually to count with a function that counts character

145
00:06:54,740 --> 00:06:57,060
 and to normalize the text before it saves.

146
00:06:57,060 --> 00:07:00,500
 Irom's law is about a different kind of edge case.

147
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:03,940
 It's the behavior in your app that you actually never promised.

148
00:07:03,940 --> 00:07:05,940
 It's named after Irom Wright.

149
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:10,420
 Iraum's law states that with a sufficient number of users of an API,

150
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:12,740
 it does not matter what you promise in the contract.

151
00:07:12,740 --> 00:07:16,580
 All observable behaviors of your system will be adopted on,

152
00:07:16,580 --> 00:07:18,500
 will be depended on by someone.

153
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:19,380
 Well, let me go.

154
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:22,420
 So let's say our online shop API happens to return products

155
00:07:22,420 --> 00:07:23,780
 in the order that we had it.

156
00:07:23,780 --> 00:07:24,820
 We didn't choose that.

157
00:07:24,820 --> 00:07:27,460
 The query just happened to come back that way.

158
00:07:27,460 --> 00:07:30,260
 Now, if someone is using our API to show products in their app,

159
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:34,420
 and they're showing the newest products by taking the last product in the list,

160
00:07:34,420 --> 00:07:37,380
 then if we, on our end, maybe add an index

161
00:07:37,380 --> 00:07:39,660
 or change the query, the order will change

162
00:07:39,660 --> 00:07:40,860
 and the half will break.

163
00:07:40,860 --> 00:07:42,700
 To them, we've broken something.

164
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:44,700
 To us, we didn't break anything because we never

165
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:45,740
 promised that behavior.

166
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:48,740
 But because they are used to getting that behavior,

167
00:07:48,740 --> 00:07:50,780
 they now think that we've broken something.

168
00:07:50,780 --> 00:07:53,260
 So this is also the kind of edge case

169
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:54,660
 that you also have to consider.

170
00:07:54,660 --> 00:07:56,300
 To be honest, there's so much more.

171
00:07:56,300 --> 00:07:58,220
 You can't undo every single one, right?

172
00:07:58,220 --> 00:08:01,500
 You can't protect your app from every single possible edge case

173
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:03,500
 because you can't even predict every single one.

174
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:05,900
 If you want to wait till you predict every single one,

175
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:07,500
 you probably wouldn't ship anything.

176
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:10,140
 You can catch a few of them from some of the ones

177
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:11,460
 that I've talked about in this video,

178
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:14,860
 but you probably wouldn't be able to catch all of them.

